Z||000000||fiero56||11-19-2000||06:33 PM||||1st, the situation:<P>The 2.8 in my ('88)Formula, is very tired, and ready for a rebuild. I bought a 2.8 from an '86SE, to stick in the Formula while I rebuild the 2.8 currently in the Formula. I decided before I put the spare 2.8 in the Formula, I would rebuild the bottom end by having the crank reground, new bearings, o/p, etc. Someone mentioned I should just replace the rings while I have the engine apart, even though I have no plans to have the cylinders honed. Question is will there be any adavantage or problems by installing fresh piston rings in cylinders that haven't been honed? I plan to put a maximum of 30,000 miles on the spare 2.8.||||reg||5
Z||000001||rodmcneill||11-19-2000||08:12 PM||||Installing new rings on glazed cyl. walls probably not a good idea.Could lead to lower compression,blowbye or blue smoke.When I used to rebuild motors there used to be a brand of piston rings (Hastings) I believe that were for severe duty that could maybe used in this cicumstance.I don't know if they make rings to fit Fiero's.||||reg||1
